Use this guide when you already work with Sass/SCSS and have an existing Jekyll site. Work in a branch or save a backup. For a built-in design, use [Choose a skin](./22-choose-skin); for one value, use [Change a color](./23-change-color).

## Preview the assignments

Use the optional Codeply sandbox to preview assignments before applying them to your site. It requires working with Sass/SCSS; you can also apply the example directly and use your existing local Jekyll preview.

[Go to the Codeply sandbox](https://www.codeply.com/p/qhEml875ge) 

The sandbox example includes **INDEX** and **CLASSICNAVBARDEMO** sections in the **HTML** panel for comparing navbar styles.

### Step-by-step example

For example, the [Sunset theme](https://themesguide.github.io/top-hat/dist/sunset/)by TopHat uses the following font import and palette assignments in `theme.scss`:

```
/*! Tophat `Sunset` Bootstrap 4.3.1 theme */
@import url(https://fonts.googleapis.com/css?family=Voltaire:200,300,400,700);
$headings-font-family:Voltaire;

// Do not disable Bootstrap grid classes in the sandbox.
$primary: #2F414A;
$secondary: #F47B53;
$success: #420084;
$danger: #f2460d;
$info: #7ebcfa;
$warning: #ff9933;
$light: #eef0f2;
$dark: #000633;
// Do not add a second Bootstrap import in the sandbox.

// Add custom Sass rules here.


```

#### 1\. In the sandbox

1. Open the sandbox and paste the font import and variable assignments above its existing imports in the **CSS** panel.
2. Run the sandbox to preview Markdown and B

